<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
  Custom genes. Kept deliberately small: the Colors are built almost entirely
  from verified vanilla genes, so this file only holds what vanilla has no
  equivalent for.

  Mirrored from:
    Biotech/Defs/GeneDefs/GeneDefs_Health.xml  (FertilityBase, Sterile, Fertile)
    Biotech/Defs/GeneDefs/GeneDefs_Misc.xml    (Inbred, for the Fertility statFactor)

  Vanilla ships only two points on the fertility scale: Sterile (Fertility 0)
  and Fertile (Fertility 2). Gold needs a partial reduction, which does not
  exist, hence this def. `Inbred` proves a fractional <Fertility> statFactor is
  valid - it uses 0.5.
-->
<Defs>

  <GeneDef>
    <defName>RR_Fertility_Reduced</defName>
    <label>engineered scarcity</label>
    <labelShortAdj>scarce</labelShortAdj>
    <description>Carriers conceive rarely and with difficulty. The trait is not a defect but a control: a caste that cannot casually replace its own losses stays small, and a caste that stays small stays valuable. Every birth is an administrative event.</description>
    <iconPath>UI/Icons/Genes/RR_FertilityReduced</iconPath>
    <displayCategory>Reproduction</displayCategory>
    <displayOrderInCategory>120</displayOrderInCategory>
    <exclusionTags>
      <li>Fertility</li>
    </exclusionTags>
    <statFactors>
      <Fertility>0.4</Fertility>
    </statFactors>
    <biostatMet>1</biostatMet>
    <biostatCpx>1</biostatCpx>
  </GeneDef>

  <!--
    Gold's appetite and expectations.

    Golds are engineered larger, denser and faster than anything else walking,
    and that physiology has to be fed. They are also raised never once having
    encountered a room that was not arranged for them, which turns out to be
    just as expensive.

    Neither is a capability weakness - a hungry, fussy Gold is still the most
    dangerous person in the room. Both are economic costs, which is the shape
    the caste's constraint is meant to take.

    Mechanically:
      biostatMet -3     -> markedly higher food consumption
      forcedTraits Greedy -> mood loss without an impressive bedroom, i.e.
                             higher expectations, expressed the only way vanilla
                             exposes in pure XML. ExpectationDefs exist but are
                             driven by wealth and royal title, not by genes;
                             raising them per-pawn would need C#.

    forcedTraits verified on Biotech/Defs/GeneDefs/GeneDefs_Misc.xml (KindInstinct).
    Greedy verified in Core/Defs/TraitDefs/Traits_Singular.xml.
    Miscellaneous verified as a GeneCategoryDef in Core/Defs/GeneDefs/GeneDefs_Endogenes.xml.
  -->
  <GeneDef>
    <defName>RR_HighbornAppetite</defName>
    <label>highborn appetite</label>
    <labelShortAdj>highborn</labelShortAdj>
    <description>An engineered body of this quality is not cheap to run. Carriers eat prodigiously and take it as unremarkable, having never been asked to do otherwise, and they are wretched in accommodation they consider beneath them. Neither trait slows them down in the slightest. Both are simply part of what it costs to keep one.</description>
    <iconPath>UI/Icons/Genes/RR_HighbornAppetite</iconPath>
    <displayCategory>Miscellaneous</displayCategory>
    <displayOrderInCategory>120</displayOrderInCategory>
    <forcedTraits>
      <li>
        <def>Greedy</def>
      </li>
    </forcedTraits>
    <biostatMet>-3</biostatMet>
  </GeneDef>

  <!--
    The helldiver's knack (docs/Opus-Notes.md, "Red deep-drilling bonus"): Reds
    are bred and broken to the drill. DeepDrillingSpeed is a vanilla stat
    (Core/Defs/Stats/Stats_Pawns_WorkGeneral.xml) and a plain statFactors entry, as
    RR_Fertility_Reduced above uses one. Both Red xenotypes carry it. Cheap - cpx 1,
    met 0 - because it is a skill of the hands, not a redesign of the body. The
    clawDrill is where it pays (Buildings_RimRising.xml).
  -->
  <GeneDef>
    <defName>RR_Helldiver</defName>
    <label>helldiver</label>
    <labelShortAdj>helldiver</labelShortAdj>
    <description>Carriers are bred to the drill: a feel for rock, steady hands under vibration and the nerve to keep a cutting head moving when the seam starts to sing. They run a deep drill half again as fast as anyone else.</description>
    <iconPath>UI/Icons/Genes/RR_Helldiver</iconPath>
    <displayCategory>Miscellaneous</displayCategory>
    <statFactors>
      <DeepDrillingSpeed>1.5</DeepDrillingSpeed>
    </statFactors>
    <biostatCpx>1</biostatCpx>
  </GeneDef>

  <!--
    Blues and gravships (suggestion 6.7). Odyssey's PilotingAbility is a real pawn
    stat - the one the gravship launch ritual grades on - so the Blue's helm sense is a
    plain statFactors entry. MayRequire on the value node (CLAUDE.md: MayRequire works
    on value nodes): without Odyssey the gene is inert rather than an error. A bonus,
    never a gate: a Red colony with no Blue can still leave.
  -->
  <GeneDef>
    <defName>RR_HelmSense</defName>
    <label>helm sense</label>
    <labelShortAdj>helm-born</labelShortAdj>
    <description>Carriers are grown for the bridge: three-dimensional judgment, reflexes tuned to a ship's response rather than a body's, and an instinct for when a drive is about to fail. They fly better than anyone who merely learned how.</description>
    <iconPath>UI/Icons/Genes/RR_HelmSense</iconPath>
    <displayCategory>Miscellaneous</displayCategory>
    <statFactors>
      <PilotingAbility MayRequire="Ludeon.RimWorld.Odyssey">1.4</PilotingAbility>
    </statFactors>
    <biostatCpx>1</biostatCpx>
  </GeneDef>

</Defs>
